  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CST, under a sunny/mostly sunny sky, temperatures are \nwarming through the 50s, heading for the low 60s across portions of \ncentral and south central South Dakota. Winds are out of the south \nat 15 to 30 mph with gusts up to 40 mph.\n\nWhile 35 to 50 mph downslope wind gusts develop this evening and \novernight in the lee-of-the-Prairie-Coteau, surface low pressure \ntroffing that moves into north central South Dakota should knock \nwinds down to light and variable. With little to no significant \ncloud cover streaming over this area tonight/Monday morning, \nexpecting (patchy to areas of) fog coverage across north central \nSouth Dakota by sunrise Monday morning.\n\nNot a whole lot of change in the overall forecast steering flow \npattern during this seven day stretch. Upper level ridging over the \nmiddle of the CONUS appears to be interrupted a couple of times by \nlow pressure systems working in off the west coast. The first system \nis progged to track from the central Rockies up through western \nSouth Dakota into North Dakota and Minnesota, keeping most of the \nprecipitation potential north/west of the CWA Wednesday \nnight/Thursday. The second system is still progged to impact the CWA \nmainly Friday night (50-70% chance of measurable) through Saturday.\n\nPlenty of warm air remains in place across the region on these 15 to \n30 mph southerly winds through Tuesday. Right now, low temperatures \nMonday night and Tuesday night could end up tie-ing or breaking \nrecords for warmth. Wednesday is probably also fairly warm, \nalthough, a wind shift to west-northwest winds is progged for \nWednesday into Wednesday night. Cooler temperatures, closer to \nNovember climate normals, are then progged for the rest of the week \ninto the weekend.\n\nWith each passing day that winds are gusting in excess of 25 mph and \nfine fuels are dry/cured, the Grassland Fire Danger is expected to \nbe elevated; probably holding in that moderate to high \ncategory/range. Will have to keep a close watch on relative humidity \nforecasts through Tuesday, as a drop in humidity of just 20 to 30 \npercent could result in an increase in Grassland Fire Danger to \ngreater than the \"high\" category.",
